Henry J Cobb wrote:
> [...]
> Because there's so much that is under the player's control?
> 
> How about replacing fixed luck levels with exercise of luck?

I thought about the luck system in Nethack for some time now. I felt
something to be wrong with it. I think luck should not be a constant
once it's built up (or once it's exercised, as you propose), rather
it should be levelled upon "usage". What I mean is, there are quite
a couple of important effects depending on luck; but should the luck
always stay positive (as long as you don't do one of the few really
bad moves like shattering a mirror)? Wouldn't it be more balancing
that whenever you "use" your luck (ie., to speak technically, if your
luck had influenced one of the rnl() events positively) it's adjusted
slightly towards 0. That would make it desirable to, e.g., throw gems
at unicorns or make sacrifices from time to time during the game, and,
as a side effect, would make the "luck management" more challenging
(which I'd find worthwhile, because exactly "knowing" about your
'luck=13' factor and "knowing" about the random results that happen
while your luck factor is in any specific range seems silly to me).
In consequence you won't get a constant lucky life but you have to do
something to keep up your luck, and, OTOH, won't be penalized if you
currently don't utilise the positive effects from your luck.

